  "body": "Stacked on #410.\n\n## What changed\n- **Workholding reads the carried stock** (`app::fixture_carried`, post gate). In a setup entered with a carried body (#317), each tagged fixture is checked against the body the setup is entered with (`rest::entered_body`). A fixture standing inside material the earlier setups left (column centres strictly inside its footprint, Z overlap beyond the 1 nm fixture tolerance) refuses the post by name: setup, fixture, origin setup, depth (`FIXTURE_IN_CARRIED`). A fixture seated on a face setup 1 machined is clear, even where the declared block still reaches through it. The check runs live, like every workholding law, and the machine table is not read. Tool and holder against the carried stock were already judged by #410's union box. This change adds the fixtures themselves read against the carried body.\n- **Lathe / mill-turn second op: refused by name, not implemented.** The reasons are in the decision record. The refusal is now one constant, `stock_sim::CARRY_MACHINE_REFUSAL`. Generation and the rest chain name it. A path cut on the mill before the machine changed goes stale, because its stamp digests the refused carry, so the sim and the post refuse that op by name.\n- **Carried-body cache** (`rest::carried_cache`). `rest::across::carried_body` is memoized: thread-local, the 4 most recently used bodies, keyed by everything the sweep reads (origin stock placement/signature/shape, lattice radius, every turn, every cutting atom's exact moves and tool atom). Names, the consumer setup's own ops (for the entered body), the viewport and the gate never enter the key. A stock without a geometry signature is never cached. The cache is cleared on `project::open` and `world::new_world`. The test-visible counter is `rest::carried_body_computes()`.\n\n## How tested\nNew suite `carried_fixtures_cache` (5 tests):\n- `a_jaw_modelled_into_material_setup1_left_refuses_the_post`: the collision exists only against carried material outside the declared stock. Gate and post refuse, 3.00 mm deep.\n- `a_jaw_where_setup1_faced_the_material_away_is_clear`: no false positive where setup 1 removed material that the declared block still claims.\n- `the_carried_body_is_not_recomputed_on_an_unrelated_edit`: the consumer's feed edit, an upstream rename and a gate read are hits. An upstream Face edit recomputes and the regenerated verdict reads the new body, so nothing stale is served.\n- `a_new_document_starts_with_no_carried_body_cached`\n- `a_carry_on_a_lathe_or_mill_turn_is_refused_by_name_on_every_path` (Lathe and Mill-turn)\n\nGated in the worktree (debug): carried_fixtures_cache, carried_verify, setup_carry, rest_across_setups, fixtures, stock_sim_document, posting, golden_cl, lathe_field_sim, setup_relative, commands_undo all pass. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised / owed\n- `stock_sim::incoming_body` (the program walk #320's display reads) does not go through the cache.\n- No heavy-lane measurement on the big fixtures. The counter pins the behaviour.\n- Fixture sampling resolves to the carried grid's pitch. An overlap narrower than half a cell beside a face can pass. This is stated in the decision record.\n- #408's `Entry::NewBlock` breaks the chain like the clip, so the cache needs no extra rule whichever lands second.\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/321-carry-fixtures-cache.md`.\n\nChangelog: A later setup's fixtures are checked against the stock carried from earlier setups (a fixture modelled into that material blocks the post by name); a lathe/mill-turn part flip is refused by name; the carried body is cached and recomputed only when an upstream input changes (#321)\n\nFixes #321\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
